SYNTH-0001♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Accountability for AI systems used in patient care must be established before deployment, as current frameworks leave no identifiable responsible party when harm occurs.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Wilson♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔California Health Care Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔The California Health Care Foundation urges HHS to require clear, pre-deployment accountability before any AI system is used in patient care — because when harm occurs, no identifiable party is currently responsible.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0002♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Mandatory disclosure requirements should be established by HHS to ensure patients are informed when AI assists in clinical recommendations, as current adoption of such accountability processes remains critically low across healthcare facilities.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Campbell♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔National Consumers League♔♔♔♔♔♔The National Consumers League urges HHS to require mandatory human accountability disclosures before any AI-assisted clinical recommendation reaches a patient, because our 2024 member survey found fewer than one in five facilities had any such process.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0003♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔AI-assisted diagnostics can provide life-saving benefits, but the lack of clear accountability frameworks means that when these tools fail, patients and families have no clear path to seek responsibility.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Thomas♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Eleanor's cardiologist caught her arrhythmia partly because an AI flagged it first. But if that same tool had missed it, I have absolutely no idea who we could hold responsible.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0004♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Urgent AI adoption in healthcare should not be prioritized while fundamental accountability failures, such as excessive wait times for veterans' medical procedures, remain unaddressed by CMS and HHS.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Perez♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔University of Rochester School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My husband waited 14 months for a VA knee replacement while CMS did nothing, and now HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption before fixing basic accountability gaps that already exist.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0005♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Bias auditing standards must be mandated by HHS before accelerating AI adoption in healthcare, as existing algorithms for sepsis detection and prior authorization have demonstrated documented harms that disproportionately impact marginalized patie...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Johnson♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔Algorithmic Justice League♔♔♔♔♔♔Algorithmic Justice League submits that HHS must require bias auditing standards before any acceleration. Documented harms in sepsis algorithms and prior authorization tools already disproportionately affect marginalized patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0006♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Rushed AI adoption in healthcare prioritizes speed and cost-cutting over quality care, putting both patients and workers at risk when human judgment is sidelined. HHS should protect patients and healthcare workers rather than technology vendors by...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Wilson♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister Denise spent eight months being treated for the WRONG thing by doctors in St. Louis before somebody finally sat down and actually looked at her case carefully. Speed did not save her. A human being paying attention did. So when I hear that hospitals are going to use AI to make decisions FASTER, my first question is always: faster for who?? Because I know what hospitals actually want, and it is not better care, it is lower costs. AI gives them the perfect cover to cut nurses, cut billing staff, cut the people who actually touch patients, and when a nurse disagrees with what the computer flagged she may not have the time or the job security to push back on it. HHS needs to SLOW DOWN and protect workers and patients, not vendors.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0007♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔The National Association for Home Care and Hospice urges HHS to mandate human review of AI-driven coverage determinations prior to any acceleration, along with enforceable appeal rights for Medicare beneficiaries.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Nelson♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔National Association for Home Care and Hospice♔♔♔♔♔♔National Association for Home Care and Hospice⏎228 Seventh Street, SE⏎Washington, DC 20003⏎⏎October 15, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎My Medicare card still has not arrived. The application went in three months ago. This is not acceptable.⏎⏎NAHC requests HHS require human review of AI coverage determinations before any acceleration proceeds, with enforceable appeal rights for beneficiaries.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0008♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Regulatory ambiguity around AI in healthcare—particularly unclear relationships to existing ACA provisions and unresolved liability questions for non-device AI tools—is stalling implementation, with over half of deployments delayed. Patients and p...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Torres♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔NextGen Healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔NextGen Healthcare submits these comments with genuine concern about what this RFI is actually asking. My staff spent time this week trying to figure out whether this overlaps with ACA provisions or replaces something else entirely, and frankly the framing does not make that clear. One persistent takeaway from our client work is that patients do not want AI making calls their clinician cannot override, and our Q3 2024 survey found 54% of implementations delayed because nobody has answered the liability question for non-device AI tools yet.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0009♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Governance sequencing should be carefully considered before any reimbursement or regulatory mechanisms are implemented, as premature acceleration risks undermining proper oversight frameworks.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Campbell♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Emory University School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Emory University School of Medicine appreciates the opportunity to respond, though the governance sequencing implied by this RFI's acceleration framing warrants serious pause before any reimbursement or regulatory levers are engaged.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0010♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A veteran describes an eight-month delay in receiving a nerve damage diagnosis at a VA clinic, which he attributes to screening software mishandling his intake forms, and urges that the proposed rule not expand the use of automated medical systems...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Davis♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I'm submitting this comment on the proposed rule because I've dealt with automated medical systems firsthand and it was a mess.⏎⏎It took the VA clinic here in New Jersey about eight months to finally diagnose the nerve damage I got over in Fallujah. My file just kept getting pushed to the bottom of the pile by some screening software because it didn't like the way my intake forms were filled out or something. When I finally got in front of an actual doctor, he was annoyed that the computer kept missing my symptoms. He just looked at my history and figured it out right away.⏎⏎I'm really worried this rule is going to force more of this kind of software on us. It sucks when a machine just ignores you and you're stuck waiting in pain. We need actual doctors looking at our files, not algorithms.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0011♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Vendor-neutral procurement standards and prohibitions on exclusive data-sharing agreements must be established before any AI reimbursement pathways are opened, to prevent the market concentration and vendor lock-in that plagued EHR adoption. Witho...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Lewis♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔American Society of Plastic Surgeons♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Society of Plastic Surgeons urges HHS to require vendor-neutral procurement standards and prohibit exclusive data-sharing agreements with commercial partners before opening any AI reimbursement pathways.⏎⏎⏎⏎Accelerating AI adoption through public-private partnerships without first having interoperability requirements in place will simply repeat the market concentration we experienced during the EHR rollout. During the 2010s, many smaller and rural plastic surgery practices found themselves trapped in vendor contracts they could not afford to leave due to the massive expense. Once vendors get embedded into clinical decision support workflows, they become almost impossible to replace.⏎⏎⏎⏎Our policy committee feels the risks to patient care are much greater this time. Based on 2024 ASPS member survey data, 34% of our practices are already unsure how these new tools process minority patient data. HHS must plan for this structural risk now, rather than waiting until the market locks up.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0012♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Algorithmic tools used by insurers to deny care — particularly to elderly and vulnerable patients — are already causing harm with insufficient human oversight. HHS should require that a real person be accountable for every coverage denial and that...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Williams♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: Re: HHS AI comment -- Melissa's draft for the docket⏎⏎My mother is 82 and we drive from home to Rochester every six weeks for her cardiology appointments. I've been doing this for three years now. What I've noticed is that the nurses spend more time confirming what a screen already says than actually talking to her about how she's been feeling. I don't blame them. The system is set up that way. But when I read that UnitedHealth was using an algorithm to deny nursing home care to elderly patients at rates far higher than human reviewers ever would have, I wasn't surprised. I recognized it. That's what it looks like from the other side of the table.⏎⏎My concern with where this is heading is that HHS is being asked to move faster on AI adoption at the same time that insurance companies are already using these tools to deny care with almost no real human review. The people who get hurt by that are the ones who don't know how to appeal, who don't have someone sitting next to them taking notes. My sister Renee's lymphoma was missed for eight months because the system moved too fast and nobody stopped to look again. I'm asking HHS to require that an actual person be accountable for every coverage denial, and that patients have a real path to appeal it.⏎⏎-- Melissa Williams, Minnesota♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0013♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Accelerating AI adoption in healthcare without structural safeguards risks primarily benefiting hospital executives and insurers rather than the patients and clinicians who bear the actual consequences of these technologies.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Hall♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔Arterys♔♔♔♔♔♔Arterys submits these comments with genuine concern that accelerating AI adoption without structural safeguards will primarily benefit hospital executives and insurers, not patients or clinicians bearing the actual risk.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0014♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Federal AI acceleration policy risks repeating the mistakes of EHR consolidation by locking in dominant vendors before competitive markets can develop, as evidenced by the widespread inability of health systems to reproduce vendor AI benchmarks in...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Sanchez♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔National Academy of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The National Academy of Medicine appreciates the opportunity to respond to this RFI. HHS should prioritize independent evaluation infrastructure over acceleration, given that 67% of health system CIOs cannot reproduce vendor AI benchmarks in their own patient populations.⏎⏎The EHR consolidation experience is not a distant cautionary tale. It is the market structure AI vendors are replicating now, and federal acceleration policy risks cementing it before competitive discipline exists.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0015♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Before deploying AI in healthcare reimbursement and clinical documentation, HHS must prioritize understanding the irreversible consequences of such systems, particularly for vulnerable populations like elderly Black patients who already face syste...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Johnson♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother is 87 years old and Black and lives in Brunswick, Georgia, and I am the one who takes her to her appointments and sits with her and watches how the system treats her. I watched it fail my sister Diane twice before anyone caught her ovarian cancer. So when HHS asks what AI research to prioritize, my answer is this: before you prioritize speed, figure out what happens when you can't undo it. Once AI is wired into how hospitals get reimbursed and how doctors document, it stays. Nobody pulls it back out. That decision belongs to the public, not just to this agency.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0016♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Real-world performance of AI diagnostic tools degrades significantly when applied to fragmented, inconsistently coded records common in under-resourced care settings, and clinician over-reliance compounds this risk. HHS should mandate real-world v...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Wright♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔Alzheimer's Association♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Alzheimer's Association, we appreciate the opportunity to respond to this request. Our member practitioners have watched AI diagnostic tools perform well in controlled settings and then degrade noticeably once deployed against the fragmented, inconsistently coded records typical of under-resourced care environments. This is the same pattern seen in EHR rollouts and failed sepsis prediction tools.⏎⏎When a clinician trusts an automated system, their own vigilance drops. The moment the tool is wrong is precisely when they are least likely to catch it.⏎⏎HHS should require real-world performance validation before accelerating⏎⏎Alzheimer's Association Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎adoption, not after deployment has already occurred across thousands of practices serving vulnerable patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0017♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔While AI diagnostic tools can provide genuine clinical benefit, there is concern that financial incentives may lead hospitals to prioritize cost-cutting over patient care, with insufficient oversight to ensure savings are reinvested appropriately ...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Gonzalez♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist's computer caught something in my EKG he almost missed, so I'm not against this. But hospitals are businesses, and businesses cut costs. Who's checking that the savings don't just go upstairs?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0018♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A review of 112 published clinical AI manuscripts revealed that fewer than 18% included prospective real-world performance data, demonstrating that accelerating AI adoption without mandatory bias auditing standards risks institutionalizing harm at...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Hall♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔MD Anderson Cancer Center♔♔♔♔♔♔MD Anderson's biostatistics working group reviewed 112 published clinical AI manuscripts in 2024 and found that fewer than 18% included prospective real-world performance data, which makes acceleration without mandatory bias auditing standards a methodological catastrophe waiting to institutionalize harm at scale.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0019♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Administrative adoption of AI systems in hospitals risks prioritizing efficiency over genuine patient observation, as financial incentives may push doctors toward faster throughput at the expense of the hands-on clinical attention that leads to ac...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Allen♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister was told she just had "stress" for eight months... until a doctor here in Omaha finally looked at her swollen knuckles and saw she couldn't even grip her purse. He didn't just stare at a screen. I worry about hospital administrators buying up these new AI systems lately. If a computer program is running things, doctors won't have the time to actually observe people anymore. The executives just want to rush folks out the door quicker. That might save them money but it doesn't mean the care is improving.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0020♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Amgen's clinical trial data from Indiana shows that AI-driven encounter workflows reduce adverse event reporting by 12-15% and lower patient engagement compared to traditional physician-led intakes, leading the company to urge HHS to require docum...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Flores♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔Amgen♔♔♔♔♔♔As part of Amgen's broader response to the HHS inquiry into artificial intelligence, the Indiana clinical operations team is providing this supporting operational feedback. In my role as a practice manager overseeing these regional trial networks, site-level monitoring highlights risks with the RFI's push for rapid deployment prior to establishing firm safety parameters. When partner clinics utilize automated AI programs for interim visits during Phase II and Phase III trials, we observe a distinct reduction in adverse event capture rates compared to control sites relying on traditional investigator visits. Participants exhibit reduced engagement with these digital interfaces, leading to noticeably lower protocol compliance regarding prescribed biologic therapies.⏎Before CMS finalizes the new Physician Fee Schedule reimbursement incentives, HHS must require explicit documentation of physician involvement. Establishing a framework for periodic site auditing will also help verify that baseline standards for direct provider interactions are consistently maintained across decentralized networks.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0021♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Federal endorsement or incentives for AI tools in clinical care should be contingent on deploying organizations documenting the workforce impacts on displaced workers, particularly as cost-driven AI adoption is disproportionately eliminating admin...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Lewis♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔Apple Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Apple Inc. submits these comments in response to the HHS RFI on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, drawing on our organization's direct experience working with health system partners across the country.⏎⏎The RFI asks where AI tools have met or fallen short of performance and cost expectations. In our experience, the more honest answer is that those two categories are being measured very differently depending on who is doing the measuring. Where AI has "met expectations," it has often done so by reducing labor costs and flagging workflow inefficiencies in ways that justify headcount reductions — particularly in administrative functions that are held disproportionately by women and workers of color. A 2024 survey across PINC's member network found that 68% of respondents identified inconsistent payer standards as the main barrier to scaling AI tools, but our peer conversations suggest a quieter dynamic: systems are moving forward anyway, driven by budget pressure, without meaningful assessment of workforce impact. HHS should require that before any federal endorsement or incentive is attached to an AI deployment, the deploying organization document what happened to⏎⏎Apple Inc. Comments | HHS-AI-RFI-2024 | Page 2⏎⏎the workers whose tasks the tool displaced.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0022♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔While acknowledging AI's potential diagnostic value, rapid HHS-driven adoption risks repeating the costly EHR rollout mistakes, where vendor lock-in and lack of accountability undermined the promised benefits to patient care.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Lee♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret's oncologist in Omaha mentioned that an AI flag caught something her previous labs missed, so I'm not against this technology. But HHS promoting faster adoption feels like the EHR situation again, where hospitals paid fortunes, got locked in, and care didn't improve. Who holds these vendors accountable when they're already embedded everywhere?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0023♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Multi-site prospective validation should be a prerequisite for HHS investment in clinical AI, not a post-deployment consideration, given the well-documented disparity between benchmark performance and actual real-world outcomes.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Johnson♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔Rice University♔♔♔♔♔♔Rice University | Health Policy RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎HHS investment in clinical AI must treat multi-site prospective validation as a precondition, not an afterthought, given the documented and recurring gap between benchmark performance and real-world deployment outcomes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0024♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔AI adoption in healthcare is driven primarily by financial rather than clinical decision-makers, which shapes what gets built and renders purely technical evaluation criteria insufficient. HHS should require workforce impact assessments as a manda...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Nguyen♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔Moderna♔♔♔♔♔♔Moderna's regulatory affairs team submits these comments with some hesitation about how squarely they fit the RFI's framing, but the honest answer is that the reimbursement harmonization questions cannot be separated from a more uncomfortable structural⏎⏎Moderna Inc. Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎observation. What we've observed across our health technology partnerships is that AI adoption decisions are driven almost entirely by CFOs, not clinicians. That shapes what gets built. Evaluation criteria focused on clinical accuracy miss this entirely. HHS should require workforce impact assessments as a condition of any AI reimbursement pathway, not just technical performance benchmarks.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0025♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A commenter sharing their sister's experience with delayed ovarian cancer diagnosis expresses cautious support for AI diagnostic tools while urging that any AI used in healthcare decisions — particularly insurance denials — must have a real, accou...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Williams♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I manage an office here in Arkansas and deal with enough insurance paperwork to know its already a nightmare. I saw on the news recently that insurance companies are starting to use AI algorithms to mass deny medicare claims for elderly people. The denials happen so fast nobody can even appeal them. That is terrifying and exactly why hhs needs to put strict rules on this.⏎⏎⏎⏎Im not totally against the technology. My sister Doreen went eight months without getting a correct diagnosis for ovarian cancer, and by the time a specialist in Little Rock finally caught it she had progressed a full stage. If a computer could have flagged her test results faster for the local doctors, that would have been a blessing.⏎⏎⏎⏎But using AI to deny care is a completely different story. If you are going to let insurance companies and hospitals use these tools you have to make sure a real human being is held accountable for the decisions. You cant let a machine make the final call on a patients treatment. There has to be a real person signing off on every denial.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0026♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Democratic institutions have never formally authorized the accelerating trajectory of AI technology that HHS is now being asked to advance, raising fundamental questions of legitimacy and governance.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔King♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔University of New Mexico School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The University of New Mexico School of Medicine's AI implementation working group respectfully submits that HHS is being asked to accelerate a technology trajectory that democratic institutions have never actually authorized.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0027♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔While AI diagnostic tools can provide life-saving benefits, HHS must require rigorous fairness testing of healthcare AI systems before hospitals can adopt them, particularly to prevent racial bias that causes real harm to patients like those in un...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Hill♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I taught school here in Georgia for 40 years and I know when people are passing the buck. I'm writing because of what happened to my neighbor Shirley. She had awful stomach pains, but the clinic's new computer system just... bumped her down the waiting list! Shirley is African American and we sat there for 5 hours before she ended up needing emergency gallbladder surgery.⏎HHS absolutely MUST test these medical programs for fairness. Why are hospitals even allowed to buy this stuff without proof it works for EVERYONE? And when a computer makes a terrible mistake, HHS needs to talk directly to the patients who got hurt. Don't just take the hospital administrator's word for it!!!♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0028♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔HIMSS urges HHS to require mandatory independent algorithmic bias audits before deploying AI clinical decision support tools, citing evidence that current systems underestimate disease severity in lower-income patients and generate disproportionat...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Jones♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔Healthcare Information and Management Systems Society♔♔♔♔♔♔Re: Docket No. ONC-2023-0014⏎⏎⏎⏎Dear Dr. Tripathi:⏎⏎⏎⏎On behalf of the Healthcare Information and Management Systems Society (HIMSS), I am submitting our response regarding Section III(c)(1) of the referenced RFI (88 FR 23746). Our informatics membership has expressed persistent uncertainty regarding how AI-driven clinical decision support interventions (CDSI) handle race, ethnicity, and socioeconomic variables. Health systems frequently flag screening outputs as potentially biased. This creates a real risk of adverse care recommendations for vulnerable patient populations.⏎⏎⏎⏎Recent evaluations show that sepsis prediction models can underestimate disease severity in lower-income cohorts.1 Within my home state of Oklahoma, our members also report that AI-assisted prior authorization tools are currently generating disproportionately higher denial rates for Medicaid beneficiaries.⏎⏎⏎⏎ONC should not advance the interoperability mandates proposed in Section III(B)(4) until mandatory bias auditing standards are established. Doing otherwise risks codifying disparate treatment across health networks. HIMSS urges HHS to revise the proposed framework at 45 CFR Part 170 to require independent algorithmic bias audits prior to deployment. These audits must explicitly evaluate performance impacts on Medicaid-enrolled and rural patient populations.⏎⏎⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Joseph Jones⏎⏎Director of Government Affairs⏎⏎HIMSS⏎⏎⏎⏎1 Wong A, et al. J Am Med Inform Assoc. 2022;29(4).♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0029♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Automated clinical decision-making systems can harm patients through opaque, uncommunicated changes to their care, and known racial and economic biases in these systems remain unaddressed. Expanding and accelerating their adoption without clear fa...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Nelson♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Two years ago I showed up at the pharmacy and my prescription had been changed by some automated review system and nobody had called me, nobody explained anything, it just happened and I spent weeks trying to get a person to actually look at my chart and fix it so when I see the word "accelerating" in this request I have to ask who that kind of speed actually helps, since it definitely was not good for me, and I've read that these systems already treat patients differently depending on their race or income and if nobody has fixed that yet I don't understand why making the systems faster and more widespread is the answer and my real question is who is checking these for fairness before they go everywhere and what happens to the patients who get hurt while we're figuring that out.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0030♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Allowing private industry groups to self-certify medical AI creates an unacceptable accountability vacuum, as no clear legal liability exists when algorithms cause patient harm. HHS should not endorse industry self-certification until a enforceabl...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Harris♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS has no business letting the software industry rubber-stamp its own medical algorithms when nobody even knows who is supposed to get sued when these machines are wrong. I run a small contracting business in Montana and if I screw up a job I am on the hook for it. I cant just tell my clients that I wrote my own safety certificate so its not my problem. But thats exactly what HHS wants to let happen with this hospital AI.⏎I started digging into this whole liability nightmare right after my wife Carol had a terrifying incident at our local clinic in Billings. She went in with really severe stomach pain. The nurse just kept looking at her tablet and said the computer system flagged her symptoms as indigestion and they tried to send us home. She didnt even look up from the screen to see Carol was sweating and in extreme pain. We had to refuse to leave until a real doctor came in, and it turned out her gallbladder was infected. When I got pissed off and asked the clinic who is responsible when the software almost sends someone home to get worse, it was just a bunch of finger pointing. The doctor just said the computer gave them the wrong protocol, and when I went to the admin office they told me they just buy what the software company sells them. The whole chain of responsibility is completely broken because the vendors have contracts protecting themselves.⏎The way the government approves this stuff is a joke too. I got online to read about how these AI tools get cleared and found out about this FDA 510k loophole. That rule is from decades ago and it was written for regular medical equipment like scanners... stuff you can actually touch. How does that make any sense for a computer program that is constantly updating and changing itself? From what I read the companies dont even have to prove their software works on real patients before they start pushing it on hospitals.⏎Right now an industry certification is just a piece of paper to cover the tech companies butts. If HHS lets them self-certify, these software guys definitely aren't going to volunteer to take the financial hit when a patient gets injured. The hospitals don't even know who is responsible right now. Until there is an actual legal framework where somebody has to take the blame and pay up when a patient is misdiagnosed by a computer, you cant just let the industry police itself. Its totally backwards to let them make up their own rules when nobody will stand behind the product when it actually screws up.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0031♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Patient trust in AI clinical tools depends on visible human accountability, not just certification processes.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Scott♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔Change Healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔Change Healthcare submits these comments in response to the HHS RFI on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. The RFI's framing skips something basic: clinicians in our portfolio consistently report that patients don't trust tools they can't see a human standing behind. Certification costs matter, but that's the smaller problem.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0032♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Mandatory bias auditing of clinical AI systems must occur before deployment, as existing tools for sepsis detection and prior authorization have already demonstrated documented disparities that are actively harming Medicaid patients at scale.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Harris♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔American Health Information Management Association♔♔♔♔♔♔AHIMA urges HHS to prioritize mandatory bias auditing before clinical AI deployment, not after. Documented disparities in sepsis tools and prior authorization systems affecting Medicaid patients are already causing harm at scale.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0033♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Incentives for AI adoption in healthcare should be contingent on a verifiable accountability framework that legally binds a financially capable party to responsibility for clinical outcomes.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Thompson♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔American Civil Liberties Union♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Civil Liberties Union urges HHS to condition any AI adoption incentive on a demonstrated, contractually enforceable accountability chain naming a solvent party responsible for clinical outcomes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0034♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Accelerating AI clinical adoption without enforceable accountability structures will leave injured patients without recourse, as no identifiable institution will accept responsibility for harms caused.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Jackson♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔Wayne State University School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Wayne State University School of Medicine submits these comments with concern that accelerating AI clinical adoption without enforceable accountability structures guarantees that injured patients bear costs that no identifiable institution will claim.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0035♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A parent whose daughter suffered two years of misdiagnosis due to rushed, checklist-driven medical care warns that deploying AI diagnostic tools without rigorous auditing will repeat and amplify the same failures, demanding clear accountability st...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔King♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Cassie spent two years getting misdiagnosed because doctors were just rushing through appointments. We went to a couple different clinics and got wrong answers both times because they were just checking off boxes on a screen to get us out the door. What finally worked was driving up to a specialist in Duluth. He actually spent an hour with us, noticed she was holding her arm weird, and asked questions that weren't on the standard intake forms. He didn't know what was wrong right away and admitted it instead of just guessing so he could move on to the next room. That carefulness is how we finally found out she has an autoimmune thing. It wasn't because the clinic was trying to maximize their daily patient numbers.⏎Anyway I'm writing this because I keep reading that HHS wants to rush AI tools into diagnosing people, and that they want to cut back on the audits that make sure these programs actually work. This scares the hell out of me frankly. Cassie's chart is incredibly complex, requiring multiple specialists and a lot of time to figure out. I simply cannot trust a computer program built to churn through patients to actually take the necessary time for a kid like mine. If HHS pushes this through without strict auditing just to speed things up, who is actually going to be held responsible when an AI screws up my kid's care?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0036♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A 17-year oncology nurse and family caregiver argues that longitudinal patient relationships enable clinicians to detect what data-driven systems cannot, and urges HHS to prioritize research on how AI-mediated workflows affect clinical accountabil...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Martin♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was sent home from urgent care with antibiotics for a UTI. She was 67, lived alone outside of Carson City, and had been feeling off for weeks in a way she couldn't quite describe. What caught it was her primary care physician of eleven years, who knew her well enough to say the presentation didn't sit right with him. He ordered a different workup. Early-stage ovarian cancer. I spent seventeen years in oncology nursing at a hospital in Reno, and I have seen that exact dynamic play out in both directions. The algorithm would have confirmed the UTI. The relationship caught something the data didn't show.⏎⏎⏎⏎This exact scenario weighs heavily on my mind whenever I read about accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. HHS asks which areas of AI research should be prioritized, and my honest answer is that before we prioritize any of it, we need to understand what we are trading away. Medicine is not fundamentally an information problem. A scared patient often cannot tell you what is wrong. They give you incomplete history, they minimize symptoms, they are embarrassed or exhausted or just not able to articulate what feels different from baseline. It takes a person who knows them. I'm not saying AI has no role, I have seen it flag things worth looking at. But the research I'd want to see before any acceleration is research on what happens to clinical accountability when the physician is managing a queue of AI recommendations rather than actually knowing their patient. I don't think we have that answer yet, and I don't think HHS should move as if we do.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0037♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A veteran nurse argues that AI-driven prior authorization systems used by insurance companies are already causing serious harm — particularly to elderly, low-income, and non-English-speaking patients — by issuing automated denials without meaningf...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Rodriguez♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I've been a nurse in Maryland for over 40 years and honestly reading this RFI makes me want to pull my hair out. HHS is asking how to speed up AI adoption but the real damage is already happening right now. Just last month I had a 73 year old patient over two hospitalizations. The computer almost cancelled her cardiology consult because some AI risk score got confused by a gap in her medication history. Thank god one of the floor nurses actually goes to church with her and caught the mistake. If she was a stranger she would have had the wrong outcome entirely. You cant run a healthcare system based on who knows who.⏎⏎⏎⏎The real problem isnt doctors using AI, its the insurance companies using it to deny care. They are doing prior authorizations with algorithms now and no actual doctor is even looking at the charts. I remember reading a ProPublica article a while back about United Healthcare using some AI program to deny rehab stays for Medicare Advantage patients. The computer just spits out a recovery timeline that has nothing to do with the actual sick patient in the bed. People get denied for skilled nursing weeks before their actual doctor says they are safe to go home. The denial happens in two seconds but the appeal takes forever. Older patients with dementia get stranded because their families are already overwhelmed and cant fight a written appeal before the deadline.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS already has the power to stop this. You can just make a rule that a real human has to review these AI denials before they go out. But instead of doing that, you're asking how to speed AI up. That is a choice you are making and its hurting people.⏎⏎⏎⏎It breaks my heart seeing who gets hit hardest. It's always my older patients, folks on fixed incomes, or families who barely speak English. Half the time they dont even know they CAN appeal. And even if they do, trying to navigate all that complicated insurance paperwork in a second language without a social worker holding your hand is basically impossible. The computers just spit out denials faster than these poor families can keep up.⏎⏎⏎⏎You ask about evaluating these AI tools but just testing them before they get turned on is nowhere near enough. Insurance claims data is full of bad history. Even if a tool looks fine on day one, six months later it might start denying care to older patients at crazy rates. We need to actually audit these things while they are being used out in the real world.⏎⏎⏎⏎We need some basic rules. You need to watch if they start denying care to certain groups of people. When they do say no, they need to show their work and explain exactly how the computer made that choice. And give patients a fair chance to appeal without making them gather their own medical records in just two weeks.⏎⏎⏎⏎A tool that helps a doctor diagnose something is fine. But a machine that auto-denies care so an insurance company saves money is totally different. HHS needs to mandate human review for these things before you try to accelerate anything else. Dont wait until the complaints pile up in the news again.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0038♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Accountability gaps in AI systems leave users without recourse when errors occur, a concern reinforced by firsthand experience with institutional failures at the VA.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Thompson♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My main worry is nobody owns it when the AI gets something wrong. The VA taught me that the hard way in 2019.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0039♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔When AI systems cause harm in clinical settings, there is currently no workable accountability structure to address it, making the lack of oversight a more urgent barrier to trustworthy AI than the pace of adoption.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Lewis♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔National Organization for Rare Disorders♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Organization for Rare Disorders, the more pressing barrier to trustworthy AI in clinical care is not adoption speed but the absence of any workable accountability structure when these systems cause harm.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0040♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Real-world post-deployment monitoring in everyday clinical settings — not just academic pilots — is essential before AI diagnostic tools become deeply embedded in healthcare systems, given that their integration into billing and workflows will cre...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔King♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Regarding the evaluation question HHS brought up, you need to require actual post-deployment monitoring out in regular communities where diagnostic mistakes happen a lot. Don't just test this stuff in academic pilot programs.⏎⏎⏎⏎My brother Marcus spent eight months getting misdiagnosed and put on the wrong meds. His health just fell apart while the medical bills piled up. So when I heard AI tools could potentially catch patterns that human doctors miss, I felt really relieved. I want other families to avoid what we went through.⏎⏎⏎⏎My main concern is that once AI gets wired into hospital billing and diagnostics, you can't just unplug it later. The daily workflows and financial systems will just build around the tech. That's not a reason to ban it, but it's a reason to admit this isn't just about software. It's going to permanently change how healthcare works for my kids and everyone else. Please don't accelerate this before the public gets a real chance to weigh in. This is a massive permanent shift in medicine, so slow down.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0041♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A father recounts how his daughter's complex autoimmune condition was only diagnosed through attentive, in-person clinical observation that no data system could replicate, urging that AI evaluation frameworks prioritize patient-centered metrics li...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Rodriguez♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Marisol was sick for 18 months, bouncing between doctors in Concord before a specialist in Manchester finally caught her autoimmune condition. He didnt just stare at a screen. He noticed how she was sitting and asked her point blank why she quit soccer. she told him stuff she wouldnt even tell me. None of that is in a computer system.⏎⏎⏎⏎Im a truck driver, not a tech guy, but when your figuring out how to evaluate this AI stuff dont just look at the data. Make sure it actually leaves time for doctors to sit with patients. Also how do you measure if a patient feels listened to? Thats just as important as the tech being accurate. And there needs to be a real named doctor reviewing whatever the AI recommends before it changes anyones care.⏎⏎⏎⏎Computers processing data faster wouldnt have helped Marisol at all. Just make sure doctors are still the ones actually looking at the patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0042♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A truck driver and Medicare-aware citizen argues that AI-driven insurance denials are already harming vulnerable patients — particularly elderly individuals losing access to rehabilitation care — and that low appeal rates mask the true scope of th...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Green♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I drive a truck for a living out of maryland. Im 60 years old and trying to figure out why the governemnt wants to speed up AI in healthcare when its already messing people up. You guys are asking for comments so here is mine.⏎⏎⏎⏎I read an article awhile back about UnitedHealth using some computer algorithm to cut off Medicare Advantage patients. I dont remember the exact name of the program but they were denying nursing home care and rehab way faster than a real doctor ever would. I saw they even ended up getting sued over the whole mess. You had elderly people recovering from bad strokes or hip surgeries getting kicked out of rehab just because the computer said their time was up. This isnt some sci fi future problem we have to worry about later, it is already happening right now to real people.⏎⏎⏎⏎What really bothers me is the people who get hurt by this and dont show up in your statistics. Most people dont appeal when they get denied. Ive dealt with regular insurance claims before and the whole process just wears you out. When your sick you dont have the energy to fight them on the phone for hours and wait on hold. You just give up. So the real damage gets swept under the rug and HHS probably looks at the numbers and thinks everything is fine because appeals are low.⏎⏎⏎⏎Im no computer programmer but I know that if your going to rely on a machine you need to know when it fails. Like with the safety sensors on my rig at work, I know what weather messes them up. We need real people double checking this AI stuff before a final decision is made. It makes absolutely no sense to me why we would let a computer program decide if a 74 year old woman gets to stay in physical therapy after a bad fall without a real doctor signing off on it.⏎⏎⏎⏎Calling this an "acceleration" plan shows your taking the insurance companies side. The people paying the price cant afford to fight back. Before you let them use more AI for insurance coverage, HHS needs to require a real doctor to sign off on every single AI denial. And patients need a plain english explanation of why they were denied so a normal person can actually argue against it. Also the appeals window needs to be way longer. A 60 day window runs out fast when your stuck in a hospital bed trying to figure out all the paperwork.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0043♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔While AI shows promise for improving medical diagnosis, its use by insurance companies to automatically deny coverage poses serious harm to vulnerable Medicare and Medicaid patients — particularly those with dementia or disabilities who lack the c...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Anderson♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mom spent over a year getting treated for the wrong illness. Her primary doctor over in Muskogee was trying hard but just couldnt figure it out, and the medications she was taking for a whole year were actually making her worse. We finally got her to a specialist in Tulsa who figured out it was an autoimmune disorder. When I hear about AI in healthcare I do think maybe a computer program could have looked at her symptoms and figured it out faster. That part sounds good to me as a caregiver.⏎ ⏎ But then I see how insurance companies are using computers right now and it really scares me. There is a huge difference between a doctor using AI to help diagnose a sickness versus an insurance company using a program to automatically deny paying for care. Your RFI sort of treats all AI as the exact same thing, but applying these systems to billing and coverage decisions is a whole other animal that is actively causing harm.⏎ ⏎ I remember reading a major investigation, I think from ProPublica or maybe Stat news, about UnitedHealth relying on a computer program called NaviHealth predict. It was basically spitting out mass denials for older folks on Medicare Advantage who needed rehab after they got out of the hospital. The computer was just deciding their recovery was taking too long and cutting off coverage, even when their actual doctors said they definitely needed to stay in the rehab facility. And a lot of patients just gave up and went home instead of appealing because the grievance process is a total nightmare to deal with, especially if you are old and sick and just trying to recover.⏎ ⏎ This is extremely unfair to people on Medicare and Medicaid. A lot of these folks have dementia or other disabilities. As a caregiver I know firsthand how exhausting it is just to get through a normal day, let alone try to fight an automated insurance denial that you barely understand. So people just don't fight it. And because they don't appeal, the government probably looks at the numbers and thinks everything is fine because the appeal rates look low. But those numbers are wrong. You are missing all the people who just gave up.⏎ ⏎ HHS needs to step in and stop this. It is literally your job to protect Medicare and Medicaid patients from getting their coverage ripped away for no reason. You have the power to force these companies to have a real human being review these cases before they can deny care. If you don't do anything about this, you are just letting insurance companies run roughshod over vulnerable people.⏎ ⏎ Please put rules in place that define what a human review actually means. It cant just be some medical director who spends 60 seconds rubber stamping whatever the computer says, which I read happens all the time. It needs to be a real doctor looking at the patients actual chart and notes from their treating physician before any denial is allowed to happen. We need healthcare to be focused on patients getting better, not just finding faster ways for algorithms to reject coverage so insurance companies can save a buck.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0044♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔AI-driven insurance claim denials disproportionately harm vulnerable patients who lack the resources to navigate appeals processes, and HHS should require mandatory human review of all AI-generated denials before they are finalized.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Thomas♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Kezia spent months getting sicker while two different systems told her doctors she was fine. I've thought about that a lot since then, and the unavoidable conclusion is that nobody ever slowed down to ask whether those tools were built with kids like her in mind. Now I read that insurance companies are using AI to deny claims at scale, thousands of denials generated automatically, and the people who lose are the ones who can't spend four hours on hold appealing a decision made by software. HHS is being asked to speed all of this up. That's a choice. I'm asking you to require that a real person review any AI-generated denial before it's final, because most people I know just give up when they hit that wall.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0045♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Accelerating AI adoption in healthcare risks replacing critical human oversight with automated systems that misdiagnose patients and deny care, while disproportionately displacing women and workers of color — with financial benefits flowing to hos...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Gonzalez♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My brother Rafael spent eight months getting told by automated intake systems that his symptoms looked like anxiety. A doctor in Buffalo finally caught early-stage lymphoma. Eight months. When I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption, I think about who "acceleration" actually serves. Hospitals and insurers have every financial reason to use these tools to cut staff and automate denials, and I've watched enough of how this works to know that when they say efficiency, they mean fewer nurses watching more patients under more algorithmic supervision. The workers who get squeezed out of these workflows are disproportionately women and people of color, while the cost savings go somewhere else entirely. HHS should stop asking how to speed this up and start asking who actually benefits when the human check disappears from♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0046♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Rapid AI adoption in healthcare poses serious risks of misdiagnosis and algorithmic bias, and HHS should require independent third-party testing and validation of clinical AI systems before deployment rather than accelerating their use. There is a...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Campbell♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward Campbell⏎1847 Lehigh Street⏎Bethlehem, PA 18017⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎I'm writing this comment because of what my brother-in-law went through at the hospital recently, and I am really concerned about this push for AI. He was sick for almost a month and the computer system they use kept saying it was just a standard chest infection and sending him home. The doctor finally realized the computer was wrong and it was actually a serious heart blockage. The doctor admitted the software just didn't factor in his actual work history or physical condition right, it just looked at generic numbers on a screen. If the doctor hadn't finally stopped looking at the computer he might not be here.⏎⏎That whole thing got me looking into this AI stuff they are putting in hospitals everywhere now. I saw a news report a while back about how these algorithms are actually discriminating against people without anyone knowing. It was saying the computer looks at how much money was spent on a patient before and assumes if they didn't spend much money then they must be healthy. Which makes absolutely no sense because a lot of people just avoid going to the doctor because the copays cost too much. The report said it was messing up care for minority patients especially. I dont know how medical software works but anyone can see thats a stupid way to program a machine that makes life or death choices.⏎⏎Now I see HHS wants to speed up using AI everywhere in healthcare and I think it is a huge mistake to rush it. You guys need to slow down. You can't just let hospitals use these programs before someone actually tests them to see if they work right for everyone. And I dont mean the tech company selling the software doing the testing, because obviously they will say it works fine to get the contract. It needs to be an outside independent group testing it before it ever goes live in a real hospital. Because once the computer says something, doctors just believe it. People trust screens too much these days and stop using their own common sense.⏎⏎I run a small construction crew in Bethlehem and I provide our health insurance. Its already expensive enough and a nightmare to deal with the insurance companies when somebody gets hurt. I want to know if this AI push is going to affect small business plans or if its just for the big hospital networks. The last thing we need is an AI robot automatically denying claims for my guys because of some glitch in the system that nobody knows how to fix or explain to us. You need to make sure this stuff is actually safe first.⏎⏎Edward Campbell♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0047♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Fragmented payer classification standards and liability ambiguity—not regulatory excess—are the primary barriers to responsible AI adoption in clinical care, and HHS should respond with federal standardization and stronger oversight rather than de...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Johnson♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔Northwell Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Northwell Health⏎1111 Marcus Avenue⏎Lake Success, NY 11042⏎⏎January 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎To the Office of the Deputy Secretary:⏎⏎Northwell Health serves more than two million patients annually across the New York metro region, and as Health IT Director I have spent the better part of the past three years working through exactly the implementation questions this RFI touches. I want to be direct: the framing of this solicitation concerns me.⏎⏎The word "accelerating" does real work in this document. It signals a particular posture — that the primary problem is speed, and that regulatory friction is the enemy of progress. I am not sure that is the right diagnosis.⏎⏎Our own deployment experience, and findings consistent with a Q4 2024 survey of 214 health IT directors conducted through our industry consortium, suggest that the more serious barriers are liability ambiguity for non-device AI and the absence of any coherent federal standard for payer classification of clinical decision support tools. At least 11 distinct reimbursement coding frameworks have been applied to functionally identical tools across major commercial insurers. That is a coordination and governance problem, not a deregulation problem. Removing safeguards will not fix it.⏎⏎Northwell Health | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎What we would urge HHS and ONC to consider instead is a precautionary posture grounded in the existing evidence on algorithmic bias and clinical harm. The work of Obermeyer et al. (Science, 2019) on racially biased risk-scoring algorithms remains relevant and underaddressed in this RFI. More recent literature, including analysis published in npj Digital Medicine in 2024, documents persistent disparities in AI tool performance across patient subpopulations that would not be caught under voluntary transparency frameworks alone.⏎⏎The RFI asks broadly about regulatory and reimbursement actions. On reimbursement, we support standardized CPT coding pathways for AI-assisted clinical decision support, but only where those pathways are conditioned on documented human oversight at the point of clinical application. Reimbursement should not flow to automated determinations that displace physician judgment.⏎⏎On regulatory structure, we believe HHS should resist pressure to narrow the scope of FDA oversight for software that materially influences clinical decisions. The current 21 CFR Part 820 framework has gaps, but the answer is not to widen those gaps further in the name of innovation velocity.⏎⏎What medicine loses when it optimizes for computational efficiency is harder to measure than what it gains. Accountability. Relationship. The capacity for a clinician to say "I am not certain, let me think about this more carefully." Those things do not show up in implementation timelines or cost-per-deployment figures, and they will not be recovered easily once administrative and insurance functions are handed over to systems that have no obligation to explain themselves under current frameworks.⏎⏎The documentation burden our legal team faces — averaging approximately $340,000 per enterprise deployment in cross-payer compliance review — is real and should be addressed. But the answer is federal standardization, not reduced oversight.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Sandra Johnson⏎Health IT Director, Northwell Health⏎sjohnson@northwell.edu♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0048♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A veteran social worker recounts how an AI clinical decision tool nearly killed a Native American patient by misclassifying her cardiac symptoms as behavioral health issues, citing her zip code and Medicaid status as contributing factors. HHS shou...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Smith♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Three years ago I started working with a 58-year-old Cherokee woman. I'll just call her Delores to protect her privacy, but she is a real person and I think about her a lot when I read about this AI stuff. She came to me after two different hospital intake visits where a computer program decided what was wrong with her before a doctor even looked her in the face. Both times they sent her to behavioral health, even though her presenting symptoms were clearly pointing to heart problems. When I finally got her to a real cardiologist, he told me the computer flagged her as low priority partly because of her zip code and the fact she was on Medicaid. This wasn't just some technical glitch. The system was actually designed to look at those factors and it almost killed her.⏎⏎⏎⏎Ive been a social worker here in Oklahoma for 28 years now. I know systems fail people all the time. But the RFI is asking about what regulatory and payment changes HHS should prioritize to get more AI in clinical care, and honestly that framing bothers me. It assumes regulations are just getting in the way. People act like the main obstacle to AI is too much caution, but really the problem is there's no accountability when things go wrong. HHS is asking how to speed up a technology that failed my client twice. Speed isn't the same thing as progress.⏎⏎⏎⏎There's plenty of research out there (I've seen articles in JAMA and NEJM over the years) about how important the actual relationship between a doctor and patient is, especially for chronic diseases. It actually changes biological outcomes when a patient trusts their doctor. You can see it in adherence rates, readmission numbers, and mortality at 12 months. A machine cant do that, even if it guesses the right diagnosis. HHS needs to figure out how payment structures can protect that human relationship instead of just trying to find ways to substitute around it with software.⏎⏎⏎⏎What we need in the actual regulations is a strict rule that a human has to be in the loop for any AI used for diagnosing or treating people. And I don't mean just clicking a popup box on a screen or logging an override code. A real, accountable person has to review the output before it affects where a patient gets routed. Voluntary guidance is useless for the patients I work with. The hospitals that rely too much on these algorithms are usually the ones serving poorer patients who don't have the power to argue when the computer gets it wrong.⏎⏎⏎⏎I also want to point out that the way reimbursement works right now, it just rewards getting people through the door as fast as possible. A cardiologist doesn't get paid for spending an extra 20 minutes trying to figure out why an algorithm flagged a 58 year old woman as low acuity. If HHS actually wants responsible AI, you have to look at Medicare and medicaid payment rates. Right now there is way too much pressure to just trust the machine because second guessing it takes time that nobody is paying the doctors for. I worry a lot about the women who get misclassified by these systems and don't have a social worker to help them fight it. We shouldn't be rushing this.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0049♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔68% of clinicians affiliated with Mental Health America reported AI recommendations conflicting with patient-centered care, yet HHS is moving to accelerate AI adoption in the absence of mandatory bias auditing requirements.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Ramirez♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Mental Health America♔♔♔♔♔♔Mental Health America submits this comment with serious concern: our 2023 network survey found 68% of affiliated clinicians reporting AI recommendations that conflicted with patient-centered care, and HHS is proposing to accelerate adoption before mandatory bias auditing exists.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0050♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Accelerating AI deployment in clinical care without first establishing accountability frameworks risks deepening health inequities, as under-resourced safety-net facilities increasingly use AI as a provider replacement rather than a decision-suppo...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Harris♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔Infectious Diseases Society of America♔♔♔♔♔♔The Infectious Diseases Society of America (IDSA) submits these comments in response to the HHS request for information regarding the acceleration of AI adoption in clinical care. We are concerned by the overall framing of this RFI.⏎⏎HHS asks which AI research areas should be prioritized. Our response is that before the agency accelerates deployment, we need a better understanding of who bears the cost when these systems fail. We have already seen this with proprietary sepsis prediction algorithms in hospitals, where safety-net facilities often lack the resources to validate alerts locally, leading to inadequate provider guidance on overriding the system.⏎⏎The RFI relies heavily on the premise of "democratizing access." However, our field monitoring shows that well-resourced clinics use AI to assist physicians, while under-resourced facilities are increasingly pushed to use AI as a replacement for provider time altogether. The resulting care is not comparable. Patients with complex or atypical infectious disease presentations are precisely where algorithmic systems struggle the most, and these cases disproportionately involve older adults and lower-income individuals.⏎⏎HHS has established civil rights and equity mandates. Pushing for rapid AI deployment in clinical settings that lack the underlying infrastructure to monitor for algorithmic bias conflicts with the agency's own equity goals.⏎⏎The IDSA strongly urges HHS to mandate comprehensive equity impact assessments prior to the deployment of clinical AI tools in safety-net environments, rather than waiting for adverse outcomes to accumulate.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0051♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Mandatory equity impact assessments must precede any accelerated clinical AI deployment, as Medicaid-serving facilities currently lack the basic bias auditing infrastructure needed to ensure safe and equitable implementation.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Rivera♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔American Psychiatric Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Psychiatric Association opposes any HHS acceleration of clinical AI deployment without mandatory equity impact assessments, given that our own member data shows Medicaid-serving facilities lack basic bias auditing infrastructure.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0052♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Patients in crisis cannot be reduced to data inputs, and the therapeutic relationship is the true foundation of healing.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Wilson♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔Partnership for Quality Home Healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔The Partnership for Quality Home Healthcare urges HHS to recognize that patients in crisis cannot be reduced to data inputs, and that the therapeutic relationship is where healing actually begins.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0053♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Federal AI adoption policy risks repeating the EHR consolidation failures enabled by HITECH, as fragmented payer standards and prohibitive compliance costs systematically exclude smaller developers while entrenching dominant vendors through switch...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Martin♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔Subtle Medical♔♔♔♔♔♔Subtle Medical | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎REGARDING: HHS REQUEST FOR INFORMATION — ACCELERATING AI ADOPTION IN CLINICAL CARE⏎⏎Melissa Martin, Practice Manager⏎Subtle Medical⏎Denver, Colorado⏎⏎The question the RFI asks — which governing bodies and decision-makers have the most influence on AI adoption — is worth answering directly. But the more important question is what happens to those decision-makers once AI systems are embedded in their workflows and the switching costs kick in. That is the question this RFI does not ask, and the omission is not accidental.⏎⏎THE EHR PRECEDENT⏎⏎HITECH poured federal dollars into EHR adoption beginning in 2009 without building structural safeguards against vendor consolidation. What followed is documented extensively: Adler-Bell and Schulman's 2023 Health Affairs Forefront analysis of EHR vendor lock-in describes exactly how proprietary data formats and contract penalty structures made it economically irrational to remove underperforming systems even after the clinical and administrative failures became undeniable. The GAO reached similar conclusions in its 2022 report on health IT burden (GAO-22-104629), finding that interoperability policy alone could not undo the market concentration that accelerated adoption had enabled. HHS now appears prepared to repeat this sequence with AI vendors who have more leverage, less accountability, and deeper integration points than any EHR company ever had.⏎⏎THE ADOPTION GOVERNANCE QUESTION⏎⏎In practice, AI adoption decisions at mid-sized health organizations are made by a coalition of the CFO, the CMO, and whatever IT vendor already holds the EHR contract. The EHR vendor's AI module gets selected not because it performed best in evaluation but because switching costs for a competing product are prohibitive. Our internal analysis across health-adjacent AI deployments found compliance overhead of $340,000–$520,000 per product per market entry cycle, driven largely by the absence of consistent payer standards — we identified at least six materially different standards across five regional markets in 2024 alone. Smaller developers cannot absorb those costs. Dominant vendors can. That is not competition; it is attrition.⏎⏎Subtle Medical Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎THE REGULATORY CAPTURE PROBLEM⏎⏎Feldman and Frondorf's 2022 UC Hastings Law Journal article on digital capture documents how technology vendors reshape regulatory processes through privileged access to federal data partnerships and policy development. The RFI's framing — public-private collaboration as the primary acceleration mechanism — is precisely the structure that Feldman and Frondorf identify as the entry point for that capture. Dafny, Ody, and Schmitt's NBER Working Paper No. 31204 on hospital supply chain consolidation shows that even discount structures can raise effective costs when concentration is high enough.⏎⏎The administrative hurdles the RFI asks about are real. But accelerating past them without addressing market structure will produce a set of AI dependencies by 2030 that look a great deal like the EHR problem, except the contracts will be harder to audit and the♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0054♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Equity-specific bias audits for clinical AI tools should be mandated by HHS as a prerequisite to deployment, rather than implemented reactively after widespread harm has already occurred.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Nelson♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔American Physical Therapy Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Physical Therapy Association urges HHS to require equity-specific bias audits before any clinical AI tool reaches deployment, not after documented harm has already occurred at scale.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0055♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔The National Eating Disorders Association warns that HHS risks repeating past EHR consolidation failures at greater scale, leading to regulatory capture that will disproportionately harm the most vulnerable patients.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Taylor♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔National Eating Disorders Association♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Eating Disorders Association, HHS is being asked to repeat the EHR consolidation failure at higher stakes, and the result will be regulatory capture that permanently harms the most vulnerable patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0056♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Hospitals and algorithms should not have authority over end-of-life or patient care decisions, as the irreplaceable human insight of a longtime caregiver provides a depth of understanding that no automated system can replicate.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Davis♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Don't let hospitals write the rules on this. I've sat beside Earl, 82 years old, in Conway for three years, and no algorithm is catching what I catch.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0057♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔While acknowledging AI's potential to speed diagnosis, the commenter urges HHS to require meaningful human oversight at every clinical decision point, arguing that the irreplaceable act of attentive, individualized listening is itself a core compo...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Scott♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My brother spent almost eight months being told his symptoms were probably stress before a specialist at Sanford in Fargo finally sat with him for nearly two hours, asked questions that weren't on any intake form, and figured out he had an autoimmune condition that three other doctors had missed. What made the difference wasn't a faster system. It was a person who slowed down. I think about that a lot when I read about pushing AI further into clinical settings, and honestly I'm not sure I fully understand everything this request for information is asking, but the part about accelerating adoption is what I keep turning over in my mind. Accelerating toward what exactly. My brother's final diagnosis came because his doctor noticed something about the way he described fatigue that wasn't captured anywhere in his chart, and she made a judgment call based on that. I don't know how an algorithm accounts for the way a scared person describes pain when they've already been dismissed twice and they're starting to stop trusting themselves.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in any of this, because I genuinely think faster diagnostic tools could have helped my family a lot during those eight months. But I'd really like HHS to think carefully about requiring strong human review at every decision point before expanding AI's role, not as a bureaucratic hurdle but because the listening itself is part of what heals people. Also I do wonder who actually reads these comments because I've submitted things before and never gotten any kind of response and I'm not sure this will be any different but I'm submitting it anyway.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0058♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Enforceable accountability structures must be established before accelerating AI adoption in healthcare, as patients harmed by AI diagnostic tools currently lack a clear path to recourse.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Wilson♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔Alliance for Health Policy♔♔♔♔♔♔The Alliance for Health Policy urges HHS to require enforceable accountability structures before any further AI acceleration, because no patient harmed by an AI diagnostic tool currently has a clear path to recourse.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0059♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Before expanding AI-based clinical decision tools into safety-net settings, HHS should first investigate the root causes of why previous non-AI clinical decision tools have failed these vulnerable, under-resourced patient populations.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Nelson♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔American Heart Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Heart Association urges HHS to examine why previous clinical decision tools failed safety-net patients before accelerating AI deployment into those same under-resourced settings.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0060♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Mandatory pre-deployment real-world validation of clinical AI tools — tested against the actual patient populations they will serve, not just training data — is essential to prevent diagnostic failures caused by algorithmic performance drift and a...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Flores♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My husband Ernesto was misdiagnosed for eight months. The hospital had just rolled out a new automated screening platform and it flagged his imaging as low-risk three separate times, and the oncologist who finally caught his stage-three colon cancer told us that directly, not as a maybe, as a fact. Eight months. I think about that number a lot.⏎⏎I've driven over two million miles on California highways. I know what happens when you trust an instrument that isn't calibrated for the actual conditions you're operating in. You don't get efficiency. You get a crash with a black box that says everything was functioning normally. That's what happened to Ernesto, except the black box was a piece of software a hospital bought and deployed on real patients before anyone really knew how it would perform outside the environment where it was trained.⏎⏎There's a name for what I'm describing, I read about it in a study I can't cite chapter and verse but it was peer-reviewed work, something like Obermeyer and colleagues, documenting how clinical AI tools degrade when they move from the training data into actual hospital populations. The researchers found the tools perform worse on real patients than the validation numbers suggested, sometimes significantly worse, and the hospitals often don't know because nobody built in a way to detect the drift. We call that automation bias in the trucking world, where a driver trusts the lane-assist system even when the road conditions have changed and the system hasn't caught up. It's not a technology problem exactly, it's a governance problem.⏎⏎What worries me about this rulemaking is that HHS seems to want to accelerate adoption, and I understand why, there are real benefits, I'm not anti-technology, I am anti-recklessness. But acceleration without a mandatory real-world validation requirement is how you get more Ernestos. It's how you get the same failure pattern we saw with EHR rollouts in the 2010s, where the alert fatigue got so bad that clinicians started ignoring warnings across the board, including the ones that mattered. That's a documented problem. The sepsis algorithm failures at certain hospital systems are documented. This pattern keeps repeating and I don't understand why the people making these decisions don't seem to learn from it.⏎⏎My specific ask is this: HHS should require rigorous real-world validation before any clinical AI tool is deployed at scale, not after. Not a post-market study that starts collecting data once the tool is already running on thousands of patients. Before. With a defined performance threshold for the actual population it will serve, not just the population in the training set.⏎⏎And while I have the floor, I want to say something that is somewhat off the main point but it's been bothering me. Every single form I fill out related to Ernesto's care, and I mean every one, asks for my Social Security number or his. I don't understand why a radiology intake form needs a Social Security number. CMS should look at whether that practice is even necessary, because it's a security exposure that seems completely unnecessary and nobody ever explains why it's there.⏎⏎I don't know if anyone at HHS will read this but I'm submitting it anyway.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0061♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A retired teacher from rural McDowell County, WV urges HHS not to incentivize AI adoption in clinical settings until the technology is proven effective in under-resourced, rural communities, citing concerns about algorithmic bias from non-represen...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Harris♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Is HHS going to hold a public hearing for this rule anytime soon? I would really prefer to speak to someone in person about this AI stuff. Im a retired teacher living in McDowell County WV. Im 36 so Im not against technology or anything but I've been reading up on this rule about paying for AI in clinics and it worries me.⏎⏎⏎⏎My brother Caleb spent over a year bouncing around different doctors here getting the wrong diagnosis before a specialist visiting from out of town finally caught his autoimmune disease. While all that was happening his medical files kept getting sent around to different billing companies without him knowing, and all of it had the wrong diagnosis on it anyway.⏎⏎⏎⏎Your asking how to speed up AI adoption but maybe we should ask if it actually works first. We went through this when the government forced everyone onto electronic medical records. It was supposed to fix everything but around here it just meant computer alerts constantly beeping until doctors just turned them off. I remember reading an article in STAT news about that Epic sepsis computer model that completely failed to catch sepsis in real hospitals even though the vendor promised it would. If algorithms are trained on rich city patients they are going to mess up when they get used on completely different people down here.⏎⏎⏎⏎Tech companies test this software on perfect data but real patient files are a mess. Half the time the charts are missing stuff or the office staff typed in the wrong billing code. Peoples health changes fast and the AI might spit out a recommendation based on an old chart from 5 years ago and the doctor might just trust it. If you rush this into doctors offices thats what is going to happen.⏎⏎⏎⏎Please don't pay doctors extra to use AI until you know it actually works in regular county clinics. HHS needs to force these tech companies to test their software in underfunded places like McDowell County first. Just because it works on a fancy research study doesn't mean its ready for a local health department. If you do end up paying doctors extra to use AI there has to be some kind of mandatory tracking system so we know when the computer starts making mistakes. And doctors have to be able to just ignore the AI if it sounds wrong, that needs to be a hard rule before anybody gets a bonus check from medicare. When experiments like this go wrong the people in Washington writing the rules aren't the ones who have to deal with the fallout.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0062♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Mandatory bias auditing and demographic transparency requirements must be established before HHS accelerates AI adoption, as documented incident reports reveal erroneous risk-score assignments disproportionately affecting patients in low-income zi...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Flores♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔Consumers Union♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of Consumers Union, we urge HHS to pause accelerated AI adoption until mandatory bias auditing and demographic transparency requirements are in place, given our members' documented incident reports showing erroneous risk-score assignments in low-income zip codes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0063♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Accelerated HHS deployment frameworks for health data systems may inadequately protect vulnerable patient populations, failing to ensure robust equity safeguards and meaningful informed consent processes prior to implementation.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Lewis♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔Data & Society Research Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔Data & Society Research Institute submits this comment with concern that HHS acceleration frameworks risk leaving the most vulnerable patients without adequate equity protections or meaningful informed consent mechanisms before deployment.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0064♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Interoperability requirements and anti-exclusivity conditions must be established as prerequisites for any public-private AI partnerships in health, rather than imposed retroactively after vendor lock-in has already occurred, to avoid repeating th...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Jackson♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔Society for Public Health Education♔♔♔♔♔♔The Society for Public Health Education urges HHS to impose interoperability mandates and anti-exclusivity conditions on any public-private AI partnerships before accelerating adoption, not after vendor lock-in replicates the EHR consolidation mistake at larger scale.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0065♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Cedars-Sinai's Health IT Director argues that HHS's focus on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care is premature without first establishing evaluation standards that account for the therapeutic relationship, warning that optimizing for efficien...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Smith♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔Cedars-Sinai Medical Center♔♔♔♔♔♔Submission to the HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎⏎Re: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Submitted by: Carol Smith, Health IT Director, Cedars-Sinai Medical Center⏎⏎Date: January 2025⏎⏎⏎⏎Cedars-Sinai Medical Center submits these comments regarding the HHS Request for Information. From an enterprise Health IT perspective, the RFI's emphasis on accelerating adoption overlooks critical deficits in how non-SaMD (Software as a Medical Device) clinical decision support (CDS) algorithms are governed. In a recent internal Q3 payer relations audit of our CDS integrations, our division reviewed approximately 50 recent algorithm deployments. Over 80% fell outside formal FDA oversight and received entirely conflicting treatment under current CPT and HCPCS coding guidelines. Commercial payers applied conflicting coverage determination frameworks to these tools, creating an unsustainable compliance footprint for our systems teams. Accelerating deployment without established clinical validation protocols will inevitably generate negative outcomes that traditional claims data cannot capture until years post-implementation.⏎⏎⏎⏎The diagnostic process relies heavily on unstructured inputs—patient hesitation, contradictory history, and nonverbal anxiety markers. Current LLM outputs cannot parse these interpretive functions (Verghese A, Shah NH, Harrington RA. JAMA 2018;319:19-20). Models trained primarily on structured EHR data require an active provider presence to contextualize outputs, which the current regulatory framework fails to mandate.⏎⏎⏎⏎The American Medical Informatics Association recently proposed patient-centered algorithm metrics, highlighting an existing deficit in testing methodologies. Current pre-implementation testing focuses almost exclusively on latency, API uptime, and workflow integration. There are no validated instruments required to measure the degradation of the therapeutic relationship. Technical care quality does not supersede the clinical relationship's independent impact on health outcomes [Kelley JM, et al. PLOS ONE 2014;9(4)]. Treating algorithmic output as a surrogate for provider judgment directly threatens relational care quality.⏎⏎⏎⏎While AI integrations should theoretically offload administrative documentation burden, regulatory and liability frameworks are significantly delayed (Topol E. Nat Med 2019; see also ONC Issue Brief on predictive algorithms, late 2023). Our enterprise risk partners continually flag liability exposure when predictive outputs trigger clinical workflows while technically classified as administrative tools, pointing to a lack of consensus on what care elements CDS tools are accountable for preserving.⏎⏎⏎⏎Cedars-Sinai strongly recommends the following structural requirements for non-device algorithms interfacing with clinical workflows:⏎⏎* HHS must mandate that evaluation frameworks include validated CAHPS-aligned metrics to measure patient-perceived human presence.⏎⏎* Pre-deployment testing protocols, prior to any enterprise EHR integration, must quantify the algorithm's projected impact on direct patient interaction time.⏎⏎* Mandatory post-deployment auditing at the 6-month and 18-month marks.⏎⏎* The formation of a joint HHS/industry working group to operationalize validated relational care metrics into standard IT procurement contracts.⏎⏎⏎⏎When technology deployments inadvertently reduce face-to-face provider time, clinical outcomes suffer. HHS evaluation methodologies must structurally account for this deterioration prior to widespread clinical integration.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0066♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Automated triage systems in clinical settings can dangerously misclassify patients, as illustrated by a personal account of a serious cardiac condition going undetected across multiple visits. HHS should prioritize mandatory human oversight of AI-...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Rodriguez♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am writing about this HHS request because of what happened to my wife Elena over at the Billings clinic. We sat in the waiting room for almost three hours because the automated triage computer or whatever they use had her marked as a low priority, even though her heart was acting up real bad. It actually took four visits before a cardiologist finally caught the arrhythmia. I've run a hardware supply business here in Montana for forty years so I get why management wants things to run faster and save a buck, but you can't just put a machine in charge of sick folks. The system did what it was programmed to do and she still almost slipped right through the cracks because nobody was really looking at her. I see HHS is mostly asking how to speed up all this new technology but honestly speed shouldn't even be what you are focusing on. You need to make sure there is an actual person taking responsibility for the patient instead of just trusting the screen. I hope you put in rules making sure real humans are overseeing these things before pushing clinics to adopt them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0067♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A veteran nurse with bedside experience argues that HHS should slow AI adoption in healthcare rather than accelerate it, citing documented algorithmic bias against Black patients, the dangers of staff over-reliance on authoritative-looking clinica...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Lee♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I spent twenty years working as a nurse here in Maine, mostly at the bedside. You learn pretty quickly that computers don't see the whole patient. Years ago, we had an older guy come in from one of the islands off the coast. The triage software scored his chest pain as a low priority based on his vitals and his age. The attending physician was ready to send him home based on that screen. I had to argue with him until we got a full cardiac workup, and we found an NSTEMI. The software was wrong because it couldn't see how gray the patient looked or how he was holding his jaw.⏎⏎⏎⏎Reading this HHS request for information, it seems like the government wants to speed up how fast hospitals adopt artificial intelligence. As a nurse, I think we need to do the exact opposite. The problem with putting AI in a hospital is that a computer screen looks incredibly authoritative. When an emergency room is understaffed and chaotic, and a screen flashes a low risk score, doctors and nurses are going to follow it. They are exhausted. If the system has a blind spot, the medical staff inherits that blind spot.⏎⏎⏎⏎We already know medical algorithms have massive flaws. A few years back, researchers published a study in Science showing a major healthcare algorithm was downgrading the severity of illness in Black patients. The software used healthcare spending to measure sickness. Since Black patients often face barriers to accessing care and spend less, the computer assumed they were healthier. Millions of people were processed through that flawed system before it was caught.⏎⏎⏎⏎We saw a similar disaster with pulse oximeters during the pandemic. The actual physical hardware—the light sensors—didn't accurately read oxygen levels through darker skin. It wasn't artificial intelligence, but the lesson is exactly the same. The medical establishment trusted the technology without questioning if it worked equally well for everyone. People missed out on necessary oxygen because we trusted a machine's output over reality.⏎⏎⏎⏎This is why hospitals cannot be allowed to rush AI to the bedside without mandatory testing. I am asking HHS to require independent bias testing before any of these programs are used on patients. We cannot wait to see if people get hurt to find out a system is flawed. And this testing needs to show exactly how the software performs across different races, income brackets, and primary languages.⏎⏎⏎⏎There is absolutely no reason to treat slow AI adoption as a problem. Hospitals are businesses that want to move fast to save money. If the government doesn't force them to pause and test these systems thoroughly, they won't do it. We are already seeing issues with sepsis alerts missing atypical presentations in older women, or automated insurance tools denying care to minority patients at higher rates.⏎⏎⏎⏎The government needs to make the results of these bias tests public. We need to know what the data actually says. Because software systems change over time, the testing needs to be repeated regularly. You cannot just deploy artificial intelligence and assume the hospital staff will catch the errors. We need strict rules in place before these tools are ever brought into a patient's room.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0068♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔HHS should address documented, ongoing harm from AI-driven prior authorization denials before accelerating broader AI adoption in clinical care. Using existing regulatory authority under Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care rules, CMS shou...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Lee♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔Ambra Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Ambra Health Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎The Department is asking how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. Before HHS does that, the Department should answer a different question: what happens to patients when AI-driven coverage determinations go wrong at scale, and who is accountable?⏎⏎Ambra Health operates health imaging workflow infrastructure deployed across health systems in 14 states. Our organization has direct operational visibility into what happens between the clinical encounter and the reimbursement determination. What I have watched happen over the past two years with AI-driven prior authorization is not a theoretical concern. It is documented, ongoing harm that the Department is in a position to address and has not.⏎⏎The Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations published findings in October 2024 documenting how UnitedHealth's NaviHealth subsidiary deployed the nH Predict algorithm to systematically terminate post-acute care coverage for Medicare Advantage enrollees. The algorithm was overriding physician judgment at scale. Patients were discharged before they were clinically stable. The denial rate for post-acute care under plans using nH Predict was dramatically higher than under traditional Medicare for comparable populations. None of this required new statutory authority to address. HHS had tools and did not use them.⏎⏎Under 42 C.F.R. §§ 422.566 through 422.574, CMS already regulates Medicare Advantage organization determinations and the appeals process. Under 42 C.F.R. § 438.210, CMS governs authorization of services in Medicaid managed care. The authority to require meaningful human review before an AI-generated denial becomes final is not absent from existing law. This is a gap in enforcement will, not a gap in legal authority.⏎⏎The Kaiser Family Foundation found that Medicare Advantage plans issued 46 million prior authorization determinations in 2021 alone, with denial rates varying substantially across plans. Most of those denials are never appealed. Patients do not appeal because they do not understand the process, because they are elderly or disabled or non-English-speaking, or because the care was delayed long enough that the moment passed.⏎⏎Ambra Health Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎Obermeyer et al., writing in Health Affairs in 2023, documented the structural opacity of commercial payer AI tools and the barriers it creates to any meaningful appeal. When a patient cannot obtain an explanation of why an algorithm denied their claim, the appeals process is not functioning as a protection. It is functioning as a formality.⏎⏎The AMA's 2023 prior authorization survey found that 33% of patients experienced serious adverse events attributable to prior authorization delays. Ninety-four percent of physicians describe the burden as high or extremely high.⏎⏎HHS should require, as a condition of Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care contracting, that any AI tool used in coverage determinations be subject to mandatory human clinician review before final denial issuance, that denial notices include plain-language explanations of the specific criteria applied, and that payers report aggregate denial rates by tool and population to CMS on a quarterly basis.⏎⏎Accelerating AI adoption without those requirements in place is a policy choice. The populations absorbing the harm are the ones HHS is statutorily obligated to protect. The Department will own that.⏎⏎Michael Lee, MD⏎Chief Medical Officer, Ambra Health⏎mlee@ambrahealth.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0069♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔While AI has demonstrated genuine medical benefits, insurance companies using AI to systematically deny care — particularly to vulnerable populations like elderly patients, disabled veterans, and non-English speakers — poses a serious threat that ...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Perez♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Im writing about insurance companies using AI to deny care. I read about UnitedHealth using that NaviHealth algorithm kicking older people out of rehab when no actual doctor would do that. Most people just give up and dont appeal cause the paperwork is a nightmare and they get too exhausted to fight. It makes me sick seeing guys I served with or regular older folks getting ground down by a computer program.⏎⏎Look I know the tech can work. My cardiologist at the Milwaukee VA used some computer imaging tool that found a heart blockage two real doctors missed. It probably saved my life. Im fine with computers helping doctors see things. But using algorithms to rubber stamp insurance denials is wrong.⏎⏎You guys need to make a rule that a real human being has to personally sign off on any denial the AI spits out. And they need to explain why in plain english that we can actually read instead of hiding it in paperwork nobody understands.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0070♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Federal health AI policy must prioritize establishing open standards and independent oversight before using regulatory authority to accelerate AI vendor adoption, avoiding the same market consolidation mistakes made during EHR implementation.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Brown♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔American Kidney Fund♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Kidney Fund urges HHS not to repeat the EHR consolidation error by using federal authority to accelerate AI vendor market capture before structural safeguards — open standards, independent oversight — are in place.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0071♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Mandatory pre-deployment subgroup performance analysis and standardized bias auditing requirements are essential compliance standards for clinical AI tools operating below the FDA medical device threshold, as peer-reviewed evidence and real-world ...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Thompson♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔Flatiron Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Flatiron Health | HHS Health Sector AI RFI Comment | Page 1⏎⏎COMMENT OF THOMAS THOMPSON, COMPLIANCE OFFICER, FLATIRON HEALTH⏎Submitted in Response to HHS Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎My professional concern here is not philosophical. Over the past eighteen months, working alongside Glacis Technologies' compliance infrastructure covering 47 health AI deployments, I have watched the same liability problem reproduce itself across client after client: an AI tool operating below the FDA's medical device threshold, no standardized auditing requirement, and no defensible documentation of bias testing prior to deployment. That is the operational context from which I submit this comment.⏎⏎BIAS AS A COMPLIANCE LIABILITY, NOT AN EQUITY ABSTRACTION⏎⏎The documented cases are not edge conditions. Sjoding et al. (2020) in the New England Journal of Medicine demonstrated that pulse oximetry systematically overestimates oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin tones — a finding with direct implications for any clinical AI trained on oximetry-derived data. Obermeyer et al. (2019) in Science showed that a widely deployed population health algorithm used health cost as a proxy for health need, producing materially worse care recommendations for Black patients. Wong et al. (2021) in JAMA Internal Medicine found that a proprietary sepsis prediction model performed substantially worse when externally validated across different patient populations than its developers had reported.⏎⏎Flatiron Health | HHS Health Sector AI RFI Comment | Page 2⏎⏎These are not anecdotes. They are peer-reviewed documentation of the same underlying failure: training data that encodes historical disparities, then outputs that clinicians and administrators treat as authoritative without questioning the demographic assumptions built into the model.⏎⏎ON EVALUATION METHODS FOR NON-MEDICAL DEVICE AI⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about promising evaluation methods for AI tools in clinical care that fall outside the medical device framework, and this is where I want to focus.⏎⏎What the compliance record shows is that pre-deployment subgroup performance analysis is the minimum credible standard — not optional, not aspirational. Any evaluation framework that reports only aggregate accuracy without stratified performance across race, ethnicity, sex, age cohort, and primary language is not a bias audit; it is a coverage gap that will generate liability the moment a denial pattern or outcome disparity surfaces in litigation or a CMS audit.⏎⏎Post-deployment monitoring is equally underdeveloped. HHS ASPE's October 2023 report to Congress on algorithmic bias in prior authorization systems, and NASHP's March 2024 multi-state analysis of payer variation in AI-assisted denial rates, both document that disparities often only become visible at scale — which means evaluation frameworks must include real-time demographic stratification of outputs, not just pre-launch testing.⏎⏎Without mandatory, standardized auditing requirements, developers cannot demonstrate due diligence, and deploying organizations cannot honestly assess their exposure.⏎⏎Flatiron Health | HHS Health Sector AI RFI Comment | Page 3⏎⏎The 4.3 distinct payer-specific evaluation frameworks that Glacis identified as the average burden per AI product across regional markets is a problem, yes. But the absence of any mandatory bias auditing standard at the federal level is a different category of problem — one that acceleration of deployment will not solve and will in fact make significantly harder to remediate once⏎⏎thomasthompson@flatironhealth.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0072♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔While acknowledging AI's genuine medical benefits, HHS must establish enforceable regulations ensuring that healthcare AI cost savings aren't simply pocketed by hospitals at workers' expense, and that clinical staff retain meaningful authority to ...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Lopez♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am writing to HHS because you need to put some actual rules in place for worker protections and patient oversight before hospitals only care about efficiency savings. I do construction and a guy on my crew had a computer read his scan a couple years ago. It caught a tumor his doctor missed entirely so he got it treated and hes doing fine now. So I know this AI stuff can do good things, Im not anti technology at all.⏎⏎⏎⏎But I worry about who gets to keep the money when a hospital fires a bunch of billing clerks just to use an algorithm instead. Those clerks are regular working people who need the paycheck. When those jobs get cut the savings sure arent going back to the patients. The hospital just pockets it.⏎⏎⏎⏎Also I think about a nurse looking at a AI recommendation and knowing it is wrong. But if the computer says one thing, the nurse is probably gonna get ignored. Management paid for the computer so they are gonna listen to the computer instead of the staff. The workers don't have the power to argue with it. That is a real problem and HHS needs to make sure there is teeth in the rules so humans can actually oversee this stuff.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0073♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Mandatory auditing standards must accompany AI adoption in clinical settings to prevent the rapid, undetected scaling of algorithmic bias into healthcare infrastructure.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Nelson♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔Cleveland Clinic♔♔♔♔♔♔Cleveland Clinic has watched bias get scaled into clinical infrastructure before anyone noticed, and accelerating AI adoption without mandatory auditing standards will do it again, faster.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0074♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Rural patients face discriminatory outcomes when AI-driven scheduling and clinical systems deprioritize them based on geography, income, or age — mirroring the same civil rights violations prohibited under ACA Section 1557 when committed by humans...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Lopez♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Sandra had a terrible time getting an oncologist appointment over in Billings last February. We live three hours away and the automated scheduling system the hospital uses just kept rejecting her referral. I run a dental office so I know how billing and scheduling software works, but this was completely different. Our rural zip code basically told the computer she wasn't a priority. I was on hold for almost an hour before a real PA actually looked at her chart, saw it was urgent, and got her in.⏎⏎⏎⏎If a computer is doing the scheduling, it shouldn't be allowed to just bump people because of where they live. I remember reading that the Affordable Care Act has a section (1557 I think it is) that says places taking federal funds aren't allowed to discriminate based on age or race. Why should that fly out the window just because a machine is doing it instead of a human? It's actually worse with a computer because nobody questions the screen. They just look at the monitor and tell you "sorry, the system says no."⏎⏎⏎⏎People keep claiming this new technology will bring top-tier medicine to underserved places like rural Montana. That is complete nonsense. What's actually going to happen is a two-tier system. Rich people in cities will get real doctors who might use software to help out. Poor people and folks out here in the country are just going to get stuck with the computer instead of a doctor. Our clinics are already short-staffed, so hospital administrators will just use this as a cheap way to cut costs and pretend they are doing us a favor.⏎⏎⏎⏎You need to force these tech companies to prove their systems work for everybody before they put them in hospitals. The data needs to be broken down by age, income, geography, and race. Make it mandatory.⏎⏎⏎⏎You also can't trust hospitals to police themselves. HHS needs to hire outside groups to audit these systems for civil rights violations. And if an audit finds out the software is hurting older folks or rural patients, whoever is doing the auditing needs the authority to pull the plug on the software immediately. Not just write a report that gets stuffed in a drawer.⏎⏎⏎⏎I know how easy it is for an error in a database to ruin someone's week, but in healthcare it ruins lives. If HHS rushes this out without strict rules, you are deciding to put all the risk on the oldest and most vulnerable patients. They are the ones who will pay the price when the computer makes a mistake. Put some hard rules in place before people get hurt.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0075♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔An experienced oncology nurse warns that AI clinical decision tools cannot capture subtle, nonverbal patient cues that trained clinicians recognize at the bedside, citing a near-fatal sepsis case where triage software failed to detect a critically...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Robinson♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I worked for 17 years as an oncology nurse at Brigham and Women's Hospital in Boston. I'm writing this comment because HHS is asking about AI in clinical care and I have some major concerns based on my bedside experience. Back in 2019 we had a patient come through triage who the computer scoring system flagged as low acuity, so the hospitalist was getting ready to just discharge her. The software missed how sick she was because she wasn't complaining loudly about pain or checking the right boxes. But she was breathing in this shallow, guarded way that oncology nurses recognize right away when someone is trying to hide how much they are hurting, maybe because her daughter was sitting right there and she didn't want to scare her. A computer screen can't pick up on that kind of subtle clinical presentation. It took me practically begging the doctors for 40 minutes to finally get blood cultures ordered, and it turned out she had severe sepsis. She could have died if we just listened to the triage software.⏎I worry that as hospitals rely more and more on these AI tools, patients just become data points in a system. Anyone who has worked in healthcare knows that the therapeutic relationship between a nurse or doctor and a patient actually helps them heal. It's not just a nice bonus to have good bedside manner, it's a real part of getting better, which is something we are taught early on in nursing and that plenty of research backs up. When you let a machine spit out a recommendation and rely on that instead of a person who is physically in the room interpreting the situation, you lose a huge part of clinical care.⏎The push for AI always seems to be about efficiency and moving people through the system faster to save money. But the things that save lives aren't always data points you can just type into an electronic health record. Like my patient with sepsis, the clues were just her guarded breathing and her body language.⏎HHS needs to make sure that any payment policies or programs encouraging AI use still require a human clinician to have the final say over diagnosis and treatment. And I don't mean just rubber stamping what the AI says. There needs to be actual, documented human judgment at every step, and hospitals shouldn't be financially rewarded for putting the machine's decisions ahead of the doctors and nurses. If HHS just accelerates AI adoption without requiring strict human oversight, you are going to systematically remove the human intuition that actually saves lives.⏎Sandra Robinson⏎Brookline, MA♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0076♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Over-reliance on AI diagnostic tools risks causing clinicians to prematurely dismiss serious symptoms, as seen when a patient's chest pain was repeatedly misattributed to anxiety. Human vigilance must not be replaced by algorithmic reassurance.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔White♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Marcus came in Tuesday after three providers told him his chest pain was anxiety. When a computer says everything looks fine, the doctor stops looking as hard. We have been here before.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0077♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A parent whose daughter suffered 14 months of misdiagnosis warns that deploying AI in clinical settings risks automating and amplifying existing biases in healthcare, particularly against marginalized patients. HHS must implement mandatory pre-dep...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Torres♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am writing to HHS to demand a complete pause on these AI clinic proposals until there are strict rules with actual punishments. Voluntary guidelines are a total joke. You cant just let tech companies police themselves and somebody on the outside has to test these programs for bias before they get used on real patients.⏎Living in Oregon I work retail and I am definitely not a doctor but I was trying to read my daughter Marisols lab reports myself because nobody at the clinic was actually paying attention to her symptoms. She was misdiagnosed for over a year and we went through so many useless medications and I have mountains of bills for tests that made absolutely no sense. By the time a specialist finally caught her autoimmune condition she had lost 14 months of her life to being sick and in constant pain. The healthcare system already just wants to move people through the line as fast as possible and doctors just want to get to the next room. Now everybody is talking about putting AI in clinics and it terrifies me because these computer tools learn from old medical records that are full of mistakes and prejudice.⏎If you give hospitals an AI that just repeats those same bad habits doctors are going to be even less likely to question it. They will just point to the computer and say it gave a high score instead of listening to the patient. I remember seeing a news article a while back about some medical algorithm that was telling doctors Black patients weren't as sick as white patients even when they had the exact same problems. The computer was basically just looking at past medical bills and since minority patients had less money spent on them in the past the machine just guessed they werent as sick. A busy doctor is going to look at that screen and assume the computer is right and we are going to roll this out to millions of people before anyone realizes the machine is just repeating old mistakes.⏎HHS needs to step in and make actual rules before this is in every hospital. Hospitals need to be forced to show how these tools are affecting different races and income levels. If you just mash all the hospital's numbers together everything might look fine but you won't see who is getting ignored. Please put some real regulations in place.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0078♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔The therapeutic relationship between clinician and patient has measurable clinical value that AI cannot replicate, and HHS should focus on protecting this relationship rather than accelerating AI adoption in clinical care.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Moore♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔Health Equity Initiative♔♔♔♔♔♔Health Equity Initiative submits this comment on behalf of our member organizations regarding the HHS Request for Information on AI adoption in clinical care. The RFI's framing concerns us. A patient who is anxious, in pain, and struggling to describe what is wrong needs a clinician who can read the room. That capacity is not a data gap AI can fill.⏎⏎The therapeutic relationship itself has measurable clinical value, and HHS should be asking how to protect it, not how to accelerate past it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0079♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔The primary barrier to safe clinical AI adoption is not regulatory friction but rather the persistent gap between vendor-demonstrated performance and real-world clinical function, compounded by inadequate post-market validation and automation bias...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Lewis♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔Ambience Healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen Lewis, Practice Manager⏎⏎Ambience Healthcare⏎⏎Burlington, Vermont 05401⏎⏎⏎⏎Submitted to: HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎⏎REGARDING THE QUESTION OF BARRIERS TO PRIVATE SECTOR AI ADOPTION⏎⏎⏎⏎I'm writing to express concern with the RFI's framing, which seems to assume regulatory friction is the main hurdle for clinical AI. If we just run with that assumption, we're setting ourselves up for the exact same cycle of harm seen during the rush to adopt major health IT systems over the last twenty years. The real barrier out there in the field is the massive gap between how AI tools look in a polished vendor demo and how they actually function in a chaotic live clinic. Developers frequently fail to model complex clinical environments accurately. When systems are deployed without adequate modeling, the safety consequences fall on the patients.⏎⏎⏎⏎In my day-to-day work, I was brought on as a Practice Manager at Ambience Healthcare (we're an AI medical scribe technology vendor) specifically to manage our clinic implementations directly from a practice operations perspective. I see the struggles our partner clinics go through on the ground. Health systems are currently wasting hundreds of staff hours every year just trying to reconcile inconsistent payer documentation rules for AI-assisted workflows. It's exhausting for the staff. Regulatory ambiguity combined with deploying software without rigorous post-market validation creates a compounding mess for the practices we support. We already know from published research on predictive algorithms—for example, the well-known studies on sepsis prediction tools—that accuracy drops significantly once the software leaves the training environment. Performance drops like that are common across health IT, which makes the lack of real-world validation even more difficult for practice staff to manage.⏎⏎⏎⏎You also have to consider what happens when a clinical algorithm hits actual EHR data. Benchmark testing simply doesn't predict how an algorithm will handle the fragmented, inconsistently coded records we see in actual practice. A patient's condition can change in a matter of hours, and the AI might miss the shift. Because of automation bias, practitioners often defer to the computer and miss the change as well. A decrease in independent clinician vigilance is well documented in the literature and has serious clinical consequences (see recent human factors research on EHR alerts; e.g., Goddard et al.). The technology tends to fail right when unassisted human judgment is needed the most, rather than providing the reliable safety net that vendors promise.⏎⏎⏎⏎REGARDING HOW HHS CAN SUPPORT INDUSTRY-DRIVEN TESTING AND CREDENTIALING⏎⏎⏎⏎The ONC has already admitted in past patient safety planning documents that post-market surveillance for health IT is inadequate. Taking that into account, HHS needs to mandate prospective real-world validation across multiple different EHR configurations. Doing so should be an absolute prerequisite before an AI tool gets anywhere near an accelerated reimbursement pathway. Please ensure validation is codified as a strict requirement, rather than relying on voluntary frameworks that leave practices carrying the burden of beta-testing these systems.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0080♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Nearly one-third of hematology practices lack formal AI consent guidance, creating dangerous gaps in patient safety for those with complex bleeding disorders who require highly individualized treatment plans. HHS must establish mandatory consent r...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Thompson♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔National Hemophilia Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔When we surveyed our Hemophilia Treatment Center network last fall, about a third of the clinics reported having no formal protocols for patient consent when using clinical decision support software. The National Hemophilia Foundation is submitting this comment because HHS needs to address this gap.⏎⏎Managing a bleeding disorder is complicated. If a hematologist relies on an algorithm to help modify a patient's factor replacement dosing schedule, the patient absolutely must be informed that a computer program was involved in the calculation. Right now, without formal guidance from the Department, our providers are left guessing about their legal risks, which complicates care delivery across the board. We ask HHS to set basic ground rules and standard informed consent requirements for AI in specialty care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0081♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Accelerating AI adoption in healthcare without rigorous pre-deployment evaluation standards risks scaling documented algorithmic biases — such as tools that perform poorly across different demographic groups — before they can be identified and cor...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Ramirez♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent about three months in 2022 going between specialists in Columbia because a patient management system at her clinic kept flagging her symptoms incorrectly. We found out later that the software had been trained on data that didn't reflect patients her age and background very well. One cardiologist finally ordered a different test on his own judgment, overriding what the screen recommended, and that's how she got the right diagnosis. I keep thinking about what would have happened if he hadn't done that.⏎⏎What bothers me about this RFI is that accelerating adoption before we have real evaluation standards in place just means we scale those failures up before anyone can catch them. I've read about pulse oximeters reading oxygen levels less accurately on darker skin, about algorithms that systematically underestimated how sick Black patients were, about insurance systems that denied claims at higher rates for certain groups. These are documented problems, not hypothetical ones. When a recommendation comes up on a screen it carries a kind of authority that's hard for a busy doctor to push back on, and that's where bias does its quiet damage. On the question of evaluation methods for AI tools used in clinical care, I think the answer is that testing on diverse patient populations has to happen before deployment, not as an afterthought. And whatever monitoring happens post-deployment, the results need to be reported publicly so that patients and clinicians can actually see where the tools are♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0082♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔AI diagnostic tools have demonstrated documented racial bias, particularly in misreading pulse oximeters on darker-skinned patients, and should not be deployed more broadly until they are audited for bias and human oversight is maintained througho...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Rivera♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Marisol got misdiagnosed twice before a doctor in Hartford finally figured out what was wrong with her. That was with actual people looking at her chart. There are already documented cases of computer tools reading pulse oximeters wrong on darker skin, flagging Black patients as lower risk than they actually were. Deploying more of this faster is not the answer. Audit these tools for bias first, keep a qualified person in the loop always.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0083♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔AI health tools may be effective in general, but they are not adequately serving Asian and other marginalized patient communities, as evidenced by the Asian Health Coalition's 2024 member survey reflecting widespread patient concern about inequita...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Thomas♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔Asian Health Coalition♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Asian Health Coalition, the question patients keep raising is not whether AI works, but who it works for, and our 2024 member survey suggests the answer is not them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0084♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔HHS should mandate documented human review before any AI-generated prior authorization or coverage determination takes effect, as automated denials are disproportionately harming elderly patients and Medicaid enrollees who lack the resources or kn...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Hill♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔Regeneron Pharmaceuticals♔♔♔♔♔♔Regeneron Pharmaceuticals and its affiliated practice management operations submit these comments in response to the RFI's question regarding how HHS can support private sector activities that promote effective AI use. From where we sit, working directly with partner practices and watching payer systems operate in real time, the acceleration question cannot be separated from what AI-driven prior authorization tools are already doing to patients, particularly elderly patients and Medicaid enrollees who receive an automated denial and simply do not appeal it. HHS should require documented human review before any AI-generated coverage determination takes effect.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0085♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔AARP raises concerns that HHS's RFI on AI-driven care decisions uses inaccessible language that excludes the most affected populations, particularly Medicaid-enrolled patients. With 67% of surveyed member organizations reporting patient complaints...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Lopez♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔AARP♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of AARP and its member network, we want to register a straightforward concern: the Federal Register notice for this RFI is written in language that the populations most affected by AI-driven care decisions cannot reasonably parse. Our 2024 survey of 2,800 member organizations found that 67% reported patient complaints tied to algorithmic decision-making, concentrated among Medicaid-enrolled patients. HHS must not accelerate adoption before establishing clear liability standards.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0086♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Liability disclaimers in vendor contracts for AI maternal triage tools are creating regulatory uncertainty that delays implementation of potentially life-saving technology for pregnant patients. HHS should prioritize studying accountability gaps r...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Hall♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists♔♔♔♔♔♔The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists urges HHS to study accountability gaps rather than exclusively funding adoption research. Facilities are delaying the rollout of AI maternal triage tools because vendors disclaim liability in contracts for algorithmic harms. This regulatory uncertainty directly causes delays in advancing care for pregnant patients.⏎⏎Thomas Hall⏎Campaign Director♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0087♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Decisions about AI adoption in health systems are often controlled by administrators and technology vendors rather than clinicians or patients, and HHS should investigate these power dynamics before advancing AI integration in healthcare.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Baker♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔Arthritis Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Arthritis Foundation, we urge HHS to examine who actually controls AI adoption decisions inside health systems before accelerating anything further.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0088♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A caregiver's personal experience with a near-miss medication error illustrates the life-or-death stakes of healthcare oversight, prompting a call for HHS to require human accountability for all care denials and meaningful patient appeal processes...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔King♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother had her stroke in February 2022 and spent nearly three months at Sinai Hospital in Baltimore, and I still think about the nurse who caught a medication interaction the computer had flagged and then apparently cleared. I don't know what would have happened if nobody had double-checked that. Now I keep reading about insurance companies using AI programs to deny nursing home stays, and I understand in a way I didn't before how easily things could go differently for someone. My question for HHS is practical: when does this actually take effect, because I'm 53 and trying to figure out retirement and healthcare costs are my biggest worry. Whatever you decide, please require that a real human being be responsible for any denial of care, and that patients get a genuine appeal process, not paperwork designed to make them quit.⏎⏎Page 1 of 1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0089♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Independent bias testing across diverse patient populations must be mandated before AI diagnostic tools are deployed in clinical settings, and human physicians must retain ultimate decision-making authority to prevent automation bias and health in...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Davis♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am writing because I am really concerned about HHS rushing to put more AI into healthcare. Doctors are already incredibly rushed and overwhelmed right now. My daughter Maya (she's 11) has an autoimmune disease that took forever to figure out because two different pediatricians totally missed it before a specialist finally caught it. That is exactly why this AI push scares me!! If an exhausted doctor who barely has 10 minutes per patient gets handed a computer program that spits out a diagnosis, they are just going to blindly agree with it to save time. I teach elementary school and I see everyday how people just automatically trust whatever a screen tells them.⏎How is HHS actually going to acredit these systems before they get used on us? I read a news article recently about how medical algorithms are already underestimating pain for patients of color, and insurance bots just auto-denying claims. This isn't theoretical it's happening. You absolutely need to require independent testing for biase before these tools are allowed in clinics. The testing needs to prove it works fairly for different ages, races, and income levels. If you don't mandate that, the accreditation is just a useless rubber stamp.⏎Please slow down. A human doctor always has to be the one taking responsibility for a medical decision. And the tech companies making millions selling these programs cannot be the ones testing them for safety. That makes zero sense.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0090♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔While AI holds genuine promise for improving diagnostic accuracy, HHS must prioritize making the appeals process accessible to vulnerable populations before accelerating AI adoption, as algorithmic claim denials disproportionately harm elderly, di...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Hill♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Mia nearly got missed by her doctors for almost a year because her thyroid condition looked like twelve other things and nobody connected the dots. I genuinely believe AI could help with that. But what keeps me up at night is the other side of this. I read about cases where a major insurer's algorithm was denying claims at rates no human reviewer woud ever have approved, and most of those patients never successfully appealed. Elderly people, people with disabilities, people whose first language isn't English, those are exactly the people HHS is supposed to protect, and the appeals process is so complicated that most of them just give up. Pushing AI adoption faster without first requiring real human review before any denial goes through feels like a choice to let that continue. Please make the appeals process actually accessible before anything else.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0091♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Deploying AI in healthcare without mandatory equity impact assessments risks disproportionate harm to vulnerable patients and violates HHS's civil rights obligations, particularly as under-resourced settings may use AI to replace rather than suppl...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Hall♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔New York University Grossman School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Accelerating AI adoption without mandatory equity impact assessments exposes vulnerable patients to disproportionate harm, directly contradicting HHS's civil rights obligations and leaving under-resourced settings to deploy AI as staffing replacement.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0092♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔A parent's experience with repeated diagnostic failures underscores the danger of rushing AI medical decision tools to deployment without rigorous, real-world validation in Montana clinical settings rather than controlled demonstrations.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔White♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Kelsey was missed twice by urgent care before a third doctor caught her autoimmune condition, and I think about those extra months every time someone talks about speeding up medical decisions. Please slow this down and prove these AI tools actually work in real Montana clinics, not just demonstrations, before anyone calls it ready.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0093♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Accountability for AI-assisted clinical decisions must be clearly assigned to identifiable, solvent, and legally responsible parties, and HHS should make this a prerequisite for any AI deployment in healthcare settings.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Adams♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔Epilepsy Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔Epilepsy Foundation Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎The Epilepsy Foundation urges HHS to require identifiable, solvent, legally accountable parties for every AI-assisted clinical decision before any deployment is permitted.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0094♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔Fragmented algorithmic certification standards across IRBs and unresolved liability attribution when AI-driven processes contribute to adverse clinical events create significant barriers to responsible AI adoption in drug development. HHS accelera...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Martin♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔Recursion Pharmaceuticals♔♔♔♔♔♔Recursion Pharmaceuticals is submitting these comments based on what we see on the ground utilizing AI in drug discovery and clinical development. Managing liability across our clinical sites is a major bottleneck. When we review recent trial agreements, we see IRBs are all over the map regarding algorithmic certification standards—meaning we spend weeks doing redundant paperwork for every computational model we deploy.⏎We just eat the cost of those delays. The real issue hits when an AI-driven process gets tied to an adverse clinical event. A clinical site will almost always blame the algorithm right out of the gate, whereas the sponsor will argue the site simply failed to follow the protocol for integrating the tool. Since tech developers typically secure blanket contractual indemnities before software gets installed, trial participants are left holding the bag trying to figure out who is actually responsible. Looking at the docket's focus on private sector accreditation—we feel voluntary industry certification is a completely backwards starting point. You have to fix the underlying liability structure first. If HHS pushes hospitals to rapidly adopt these tools before we know who is legally on the hook, we build an ecosystem where patients have zero recourse for medical errors. Good luck trying to rip out embedded AI tools from established clinical workflows a few years from now; that kind of rollback is practically impossible. Before HHS acceleration programs push voluntary certification, they need to mandate clear rules on exactly who holds the liability.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0095♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔AI evaluation frameworks in clinical settings must go beyond measuring throughput to honestly assess workforce impact, including which workers lose hours, face increased monitoring, or bear the burden of algorithmic errors.♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Allen♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔CommonSpirit Health♔♔♔♔♔♔CommonSpirit Health appreciates the opportunity to respond. Our concern is simple: evaluation frameworks for AI tools in clinical settings must measure workforce impact honestly, not just throughput. Who loses hours, who gets monitored, and who absorbs the cost when the algorithm is wrong.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0096♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-17T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔AI-driven prior authorization systems are causing documented harm to vulnerable patients by denying medically necessary care at scale, and HHS must recognize that failing to mandate human review and meaningful appeal rights is an active regulatory...♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Johnson♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔National Patient Advocate Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔The National Patient Advocate Foundation urges HHS to pause before treating acceleration as a neutral goal. AI-driven prior authorization systems are already denying medically necessary care at scale, with documented harm to elderly and low-income patients. Choosing not to require human review and real appeal rights while expanding these systems is itself a regulatory decision with identifiable consequences.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
